---
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import Sidebar from "../components/Sidebar.astro";
---

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>Beginner Guide - Motto Immortal</title>
</head>
<body>
  <Sidebar />

  <!-- HERO SECTION -->
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero-overlay"></div>
    <div class="hero-text">
      <span class="hero-tag">New Player</span>
      <h1 class="hero-headline">Beginner Guide</h1>
      <p class="hero-sub">Everything you need to know to start strong in Motto Immortal</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- MAIN CONTENT -->
  <main class="main-content">

    <!-- TABLE OF CONTENTS -->
    <div class="content-card toc-card">
      <h2>📋 What's in This Guide</h2>
      <div class="toc-grid">
        <a href="#early-heroes" class="toc-item">
          <span class="toc-icon">⚔️</span>
          <span>Best Early Game Heroes</span>
        </a>
        <a href="#relics" class="toc-item">
          <span class="toc-icon">🔮</span>
          <span>Relic Priority</span>
        </a>
        <a href="#zodiac" class="toc-item">
          <span class="toc-icon">♈</span>
          <span>Zodiac Selection</span>
        </a>
        <a href="#pvp-normal" class="toc-item">
          <span class="toc-icon">🏆</span>
          <span>Normal Arena Teams</span>
        </a>
        <a href="#pvp-triple" class="toc-item">
          <span class="toc-icon">⚡</span>
          <span>Triple Duel (Advanced)</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- INTRO -->
    <div class="content-card">
      <h2>What This Guide Covers</h2>
      <p>
        This guide is built for new players who want a strong foundation without wasting rare resources.
        Everything here is based on <strong>game mechanics</strong>: items, stats, skills, relic breakpoints, suit advantage,
        and why certain heroes scale better in the early-to-mid game.
      </p>

      <h3>Progression Phases</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Early Game:</strong> 1 main team (5 heroes) that clears Odyssey + performs in Torment Rift.</li>
        <li><strong>Mid Game:</strong> 2–3 teams (you start specializing: PvE bosses / Odyssey / PvP).</li>
        <li><strong>Endgame:</strong> 4–5 optimized teams, relic breakpoints, bonds, and event-driven upgrades.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><em>Key idea:</em> build a strong PvE core first. PvE unlocks the resources that make everything else easier.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- CORE EARLY GAME PRIORITIES -->
    <div class="content-card">
      <h2>Early Game Priorities (Do This First)</h2>

      <h3>1) Push Odyssey (Campaign) for Undying Flame</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Undying Flame</strong> is the most important early progression item because it upgrades your Shrine.
        Shrine upgrades increase long-term account power and your ability to farm better gear.
      </p>

      <h3>2) Compete in Torment Rift for Calamity Tier</h3>
      <p>
        The second most valuable early item is <strong>Calamity Tier</strong> (Torment Rift rewards).
        It upgrades your <strong>Beacon Tree</strong> which grants <strong>account-wide stat bonuses</strong> to all heroes.
      </p>
      <div class="team-example">
        <h4>Why Beacon Tree matters</h4>
        <p>
          Beacon bonuses apply in <strong>all game modes</strong>. Even if the meta changes, Beacon investment stays valuable.
          This is one of the best “never wasted” progress systems for F2P / low spenders.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3>3) Get Creation Essence (Beacon Tree Upgrades)</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Creation Essence</strong> is used to level the Beacon Tree. When upgraded, it strengthens even low-copy heroes
        because the bonus is shared across your whole roster.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- TOPIC 1: EARLY GAME HERO VALUE -->
    <div class="content-card" id="early-heroes">
      <h2>1) Most Valuable Heroes in the Early Game (And Why)</h2>

      <h3>The “Versatile PvE Core” (Recommended First Investments)</h3>
      <p>
        If you are F2P / low spender, prioritize heroes that perform well in <strong>multiple modes</strong>:
        Odyssey, Boss Hunts (Torment Rift), and Spire. These carry your account progression.
      </p>

      <div class="team-example">
        <h4>Basic hero recommendations</h4>
        <p><strong>Fengyi</strong> • <strong>Poseidon</strong> • <strong>Bastet</strong> • <strong>Mumus</strong> • <strong>Hela</strong> • <strong>Nemesis</strong></p>
      </div>
      <h4>Why these heroes?</h4>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Fengyi:</strong> Fengyi is one of the most efficient early investments in the game. A Divine Fangi with a 
      Level 30 relic can dominate PvP and performs well across nearly all game modes. 
      Build this hero early and you will maintain a strong position throughout your progression.
    </li>
            <li><strong>Poseidon:</strong>  Poseidon is a frontliner with one of the best ultimate skill timings in the game — he casts 
      his AOE crowd control within the first few seconds of battle. This early cast can decide the 
      outcome of fights before they even develop. He also provides a large HP boost to the team on cast.
      Best-in-slot front line for most beginner and mid-game teams.
    </li>
            <li><strong>Bastet:</strong>Bastet targets high-damage enemy units and has a dodge mechanic that makes her incredibly 
      hard to punish. Every dodge she performs also deals additional damage back. 
      She is especially strong against carry-heavy enemy teams.
    </li>
            <li><strong>Numus:</strong> Mumus brings a Taunt mechanic that forces enemies to target her, protecting your key 
      damage dealers. She pairs exceptionally well with units like Bastet and Isis. 
      A great support investment that immediately improves your team's survivability.
    </li>
            <li><strong>Hela:</strong>  Hela is a burst damage dealer with enormous late-fight payoff. When paired with Yuelao 
      and given a few seconds to survive, she can obliterate entire enemy teams. 
      She requires some investment to reach her potential but is a worthwhile early pickup.
    </li>
            <li><strong>Nemesis:</strong> Nemesis becomes a dominant force at max relic awakening. She is a strong early-game pick 
      that keeps improving as you invest in her relic — making her a great long-term project 
      you can start immediately.</li>
          </ul>

      <div class="team-example">
        <h4>High-Value Early Core (Better heroes)</h4>
        <p><strong>Isis</strong> • <strong>Hladgunnr</strong> • <strong>Caishen</strong> • <strong>Yuelao</strong> • <strong>Dionysus</strong></p>
      </div>

      <h4>Why these heroes?</h4>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Isis:</strong> Core PvE damage dealer that scales hard with gear investment. Strong in bosses, Spire, and progression.</li>
        <li><strong>Hladgunnr:</strong> Consistent damage and very strong PvE coverage; works across multiple modes.</li>
        <li><strong>Caishen:</strong> Improves team rotation reliability (energy economy). Rotation stability is often the difference between clearing and failing.</li>
        <li><strong>Yuelao:</strong> Utility/support that helps consistent performance across game modes.</li>
        <li><strong>Dionysus:</strong> Key synergy enabler: provides <strong>ATK SPD</strong> which massively increases the output of certain carries (notably Isis).</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Why this one? (Example: Isis + Dionysus)</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Isis</strong> is a carry that benefits heavily from <strong>ATK SPD</strong>. Dionysus provides ATK SPD,
        meaning Isis triggers more damage events and cycles faster. This increases overall damage and consistency in both PvE and PvP.
      </p>
      <h3>🌟 Star Heroes — Who is Worth It at 1 Copy?</h3>
        <div class="team-example">
          <p>
            <strong>Good at base copy (Legendary):</strong> Nuwa, Amun Ra, Nyx, Dionysus — these provide 
            immediate value and don't require heavy investment to contribute.<br/><br/>
            <strong>Require heavy investment:</strong> Zeus, Nezha — hold off on these until you have the 
            resources to fully build them. They underperform at low investment.
          </p>
        </div>

      <h3>Early Game “Rules” That Decide Fights</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Do not level multiple heroes at once. Pick 5–6 core heroes and max them before touching others. 
      A fully-built Fangi or Poseidon beats a half-built team of ten heroes every time.
    </li>
        <li><strong>Suit advantage:</strong> always check the enemy suit and match the advantage (example: Hearts deal more damage vs Spades).</li>
        <li><strong>Link skills:</strong> build teams where skills amplify each other (not five random strong units).</li>
        <li><strong>Level thresholds matter:</strong> reaching breakpoints (like unlocking new passives via resonance level) can spike power.</li>
        <li><strong>Gear priority:</strong> put best gear first on your top two PvE damage dealers (commonly Isis + Hladgunnr).</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- TOTEMS (TEAM SLOT) + SUITS -->
    <div class="content-card">
      <h2>Totems & Suit Advantage (Two Early Game Multipliers)</h2>

      <h3>Suit Advantage: Always Check It</h3>
      <p>
        In bosses and many PvE fights, suit advantage is a straightforward damage multiplier.
        If the boss is <strong>Spades</strong>, use <strong>Hearts</strong> to deal more damage and progress faster.
      </p>

      <h3>Totems: Choose Based on Your Team Composition</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Keystone of Wonders:</strong> only when your team has <strong>many different suits</strong>.
          It scales with “suits owned” and becomes better when your roster is diverse.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Horn of Potency:</strong> a safe early default when you have only 1–2 suits in your team
          (simple, consistent team-wide ATK boost).
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- TOPIC 2: RELIC PRIORITY -->
    <div class="content-card" id="relics">
      <h2>2) Which Relics Should Be Upgraded First (And Why)</h2>

      <h3>Relic Breakpoints: Don’t Upgrade Randomly</h3>
      <p>
        Relics have major breakpoints. Some heroes are already usable with relic unactivated,
        while others only become “real” heroes at certain relic levels.
        As F2P / low spender, your goal is to hit <strong>the minimum effective breakpoint</strong> first.
      </p>

      <div class="team-example">
        <h4>Relic Philosophy</h4>
        <p>
          Upgrade relics for heroes that help you place higher in <strong>Torment Rift</strong> and clear <strong>Odyssey</strong>.
          Better ranks = better rewards = faster account growth.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3>Priority Order (Practical Early Game)</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Boss Hunt / PvE Core Carry Relics (first):</strong>
          your main PvE damage dealers and key supports used in Torment Rift.
          <br />
          <em>Example from the relic guide:</em> <strong>Isis</strong> is recommended to reach <strong>Relic Level 20</strong> early for full usability.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Key PvP/PvE timing relics (second):</strong>
          heroes whose relic changes the opening seconds of battle.
          <br />
          <em>Example:</em> <strong>Poseidon</strong> is valuable around <strong>Relic Level 10</strong> because it enables early casting patterns that can swing fights.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Premium / endgame relic investments (later):</strong>
          pushing relics toward level 30 and beyond becomes expensive. Do it when your income supports it.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h3>Suggested Early Relic Targets (From the transcript’s guide)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Isis:</strong> aim toward <strong>Relic 20</strong> early (core PvE investment).</li>
        <li><strong>Poseidon:</strong> strong at <strong>Relic 10</strong> due to early fight impact (PvP + certain PvE).</li>
        <li><strong>Tefnut:</strong> recommended to become “fully usable” around <strong>Relic 20</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>High-end celestial units:</strong> often scale best at <strong>Relic 30</strong> (resource-heavy, do later unless you’re funded).</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Resource Reminder (F2P Reality)</h3>
      <p>
        Don’t force ultra-rare relic materials early. Focus on the relic materials you can consistently earn via
        Torment Rift rankings, milestones, and events.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- TOPIC 3: ZODIAC CHOICE -->
    <div class="content-card" id="zodiac">
      <h2>3) Which Zodiac Should Beginners Choose at the Start?</h2>

      <h3>The Rule: Choose Based on Bonds First</h3>
      <p>
        The best Zodiac for a beginner depends on your <strong>Bond bonuses</strong>.
        Bonds can grant stats like <strong>HP +5%</strong>, <strong>ATK +5%</strong>, or <strong>starting Energy +200</strong>.
        Before switching, check which bonds you can maximize.
      </p>

      <div class="warning-box">
        <h3>Important: Switching costs gems</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Switching Zodiac can cost around <strong>999 gems</strong> each time.</li>
          <li>So pick a Zodiac you can commit to early unless you have a plan.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h3>Recommended Beginner Picks (Utility-Based)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Gemini (recommended default):</strong>
          has an AoE leap that deals damage and provides a <strong>stun</strong>. Great for stage clearing and usable in PvP.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Pisces (control option):</strong>
          has vortex/pull control and can gain additional stuns as relic levels increase. Great when you need grouping + CC.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Capricorn (tactical option):</strong>
          valued for “swap/pull” style utility in difficult stages (helps remove backline threats).
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>What makes the Zodiac-selected hero different compared to other purple heroes?</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>It’s flexible:</strong> you can switch between Zodiac forms (Gemini/Pisces/etc.) depending on what you need.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Relic upgrades carry over:</strong> if you upgrade the Zodiac relic, that relic level is inherited when you switch forms.
          This makes Zodiac relic investment uniquely efficient.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Bonds add extra stats:</strong> when you activate Bond gifts for multiple Zodiac forms, you gain additional stats and utility.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Why this one? (Simple beginner answer)</h3>
      <p>
        Choose the Zodiac whose <strong>Bond bonus</strong> you can realistically activate and whose skill solves your current problem:
        <strong>Gemini</strong> for general AoE stun value, <strong>Pisces</strong> for pull/CC setups, <strong>Capricorn</strong> for tactical repositioning.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- PVP NORMAL ARENA -->
    <div class="content-card" id="pvp-normal">
      <h2>PvP Team – Normal Arena</h2>

      <h3>Best Reference Setup (Beginner-Friendly) and Why</h3>
      <p>
        Your Arena goal early is not “perfect meta.” It’s <strong>consistent wins</strong> and <strong>stable defense</strong>
        while you continue building PvE income.
      </p>

      <div class="team-example">
        <h4>Reference Setup (balanced + accessible)</h4>
        <p><strong>Mumus</strong> (Frontline / Taunt) • <strong>Poseidon</strong> (Early CC) • <strong>Dionysus</strong> (ATK SPD) • <strong>Isis</strong> (Carry DPS) • <strong>Caishen</strong> (Rotation stability)</p>
      </div>

      <h4>Why it’s chosen</h4>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Mumus:</strong> buys time (taunt + frontline stability).</li>
        <li><strong>Poseidon:</strong> early-cast CC can decide fights before the enemy stabilizes.</li>
        <li><strong>Dionysus:</strong> ATK SPD increases carry output and helps your team snowball.</li>
        <li><strong>Isis:</strong> carry DPS that scales with investment (your gear doesn’t get “wasted” later).</li>
        <li><strong>Caishen:</strong> improves rotations and makes your plan consistent instead of RNG-heavy.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Hero Synergy Explanation (Clear & Concrete)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Dionysus → Isis:</strong> ATK SPD increases Isis’ damage throughput.</li>
        <li><strong>Mumus → Backline:</strong> taunt reduces pressure on your carry/supports.</li>
        <li><strong>Poseidon → Team:</strong> early disruption gives your carry time to ramp and cast.</li>
        <li><strong>Caishen → Rotations:</strong> more consistent energy economy = more consistent ult cycles.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="warning-box">
        <h3>Common Arena mistakes</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Building only DPS and no frontline (you lose before your carry can cast).</li>
          <li>Ignoring early-cast control (you get snowballed).</li>
          <li>Over-investing in PvP early (PvE income is still the real growth engine).</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- LINEUPS BY STAGE -->
    <div class="content-card">
      <h2>Suggested Lineups: Beginner, Midgame, Endgame</h2>

      <h3>Beginner (1 main team)</h3>
      <div class="team-example">
        <h4>Beginner Core</h4>
        <p><strong>Isis</strong> • <strong>Hladgunnr</strong> • <strong>Caishen</strong> • <strong>Yuelao</strong> • <strong>Dionysus</strong></p>
      </div>
      <ul>
        <li>Optimized for <strong>Odyssey progression</strong> + <strong>Torment Rift ranking</strong>.</li>
        <li>Prioritize gear on <strong>Isis</strong> and <strong>Hladgunnr</strong>.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Midgame (2–3 teams start)</h3>
      <p>Split your roster by purpose:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Team 1 (PvE Boss):</strong> Carry + rotation support + survivability</li>
        <li><strong>Team 2 (Odyssey):</strong> control/pull tools to remove backline threats</li>
        <li><strong>Team 3 (PvP):</strong> early CC + anti-carry pressure</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="team-example">
        <h4>Midgame Example (PvE Boss Team)</h4>
        <p><strong>Isis</strong> • <strong>Hladgunnr</strong> • <strong>Dionysus</strong> • <strong>Caishen</strong> • <strong>Tefnut</strong></p>
      </div>

      <h3>Endgame (4–5 teams, premium units)</h3>
      <p>
        Endgame teams depend on your celestial pulls and relic economy. In PvP references,
        top-tier celestial heroes commonly include units like <strong>Zeus</strong>, <strong>Nyx</strong>, <strong>Dionysus</strong>, <strong>Nuwa</strong>, and <strong>Amun Ra</strong>,
        while powerful non-celestial pieces like <strong>Poseidon</strong>, <strong>Bastet</strong>, and <strong>Hladgunnr</strong> remain relevant.
      </p>
      <div class="team-example">
        <h4>Endgame Example (Arena Pressure)</h4>
        <p><strong>Poseidon</strong> • <strong>Bastet</strong> • <strong>Mumus</strong> • <strong>Dionysus</strong> • <strong>(Celestial Carry Slot)</strong></p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- TRIPLE DUEL -->
    <div class="content-card" id="pvp-triple">
      <h2>PvP Team – Triple Duel (Advanced Arena)</h2>

      <h3>How to Think About Triple Duel</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>It’s a <strong>drafting problem</strong>: you need 3 functional teams, not 1 perfect team.</li>
        <li>Don’t spread your best items evenly. Put your best gear where it wins the hardest matchups.</li>
        <li>Build one “anchor team” that is the most reliable win condition.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Beginner Triple Duel Plan (Simple)</h3>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Team A (Anchor):</strong> Your strongest PvE carry team adapted for PvP.</li>
        <li><strong>Team B (Control):</strong> CC + disruption to steal wins.</li>
        <li><strong>Team C (Stall):</strong> frontline + survivability to outlast weak opponents.</li>
      </ol>

      <div class="team-example">
        <h4>Recommended Setups (generic templates)</h4>
        <p><strong>Team A (Anchor):</strong> Mumus • Dionysus • Isis • Caishen • Poseidon</p>
        <p><strong>Team B (Control):</strong> Khepri/Prometheus • Pisces/Gemini Zodiac • Poseidon • Support • DPS</p>
        <p><strong>Team C (Stall):</strong> Tanks/Immortality tools • shields/heals • one consistent DPS</p>
      </div>

      <h3>Tips That Win Matches</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Know enemy threats:</strong> certain backliners can wipe teams if left alone—use pull/swap tools when needed.</li>
        <li><strong>Position matters:</strong> protect your key carry; don’t waste protection on heroes that naturally move/free-cast.</li>
        <li><strong>Use Totems correctly:</strong> Keystone of Wonders is best when your team has many suits; otherwise default to stable ATK options.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- FINAL CHECKLIST -->
    <div class="content-card tips-card">
      <h2>Beginner Checklist (Fast)</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Push <strong>Odyssey</strong> to farm <strong>Undying Flame</strong> → upgrade Shrine.</li>
        <li>Compete in <strong>Torment Rift</strong> for <strong>Calamity Tier</strong> → upgrade Beacon Tree.</li>
        <li>Invest gear first into <strong>Isis + Hladgunnr</strong> (your PvE engines).</li>
        <li>Build synergy, not random power: <strong>Dionysus</strong> supports carries that scale with ATK SPD.</li>
        <li>Upgrade relics to breakpoints (don’t max everything): <strong>Isis ~20</strong>, <strong>Poseidon ~10</strong>, <strong>Tefnut ~20</strong> as a practical track.</li>
        <li>Pick Zodiac based on <strong>Bonds</strong> + utility: Gemini (stun) / Pisces (pull+CC) / Capricorn (tactical).</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

  </main>

  <Footer />
</body>
</html>

<style>
/* RESET & BASE */
html, body {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  font-family: "Spectral", "Roboto", system-ui, sans-serif;
  background:
    radial-gradient(ellipse at 30% -10%, rgba(120,90,200,0.15), transparent 60%),
    radial-gradient(ellipse at 80% 20%, rgba(255,215,100,0.08), transparent 55%),
    linear-gradient(to bottom, #0d0b10, #07060c);
  color: #e6e6eb;
  min-height: 100vh;
}

body {
  margin-left: 80px;
}

@media (max-width: 768px) {
  body {
    margin-left: 0;
    margin-bottom: 60px;
  }
}

/* HERO SECTION */
.hero {
  position: relative;
  width: 100%;
  height: 360px;
  background-image: url("/bg.jpg");
  background-size: cover;
  background-position: center;
  background-attachment: fixed;
  display: flex;
  align-items: flex-end;
  overflow: hidden;
}

.hero-overlay {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  background: linear-gradient(
    to top,
    rgba(7, 6, 12, 0.98) 0%,
    rgba(7, 6, 12, 0.6) 50%,
    rgba(7, 6, 12, 0.2) 100%
  );
  pointer-events: none;
}

.hero-text {
  position: relative;
  z-index: 1;
  padding: 0 48px 40px;
}

.hero-tag {
  display: inline-block;
  background: rgba(168, 85, 247, 0.3);
  border: 1px solid rgba(168, 85, 247, 0.6);
  color: #c4b5fd;
  font-size: 0.75rem;
  font-weight: 600;
  letter-spacing: 0.12em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  padding: 4px 12px;
  border-radius: 20px;
  margin-bottom: 12px;
}

.hero-headline {
  margin: 0 0 8px;
  font-size: 3.5rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  color: #fff;
  text-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.8);
  line-height: 1.1;
}

.hero-sub {
  margin: 0;
  font-size: 1.1rem;
  color: rgba(230, 230, 235, 0.7);
}

@media (max-width: 768px) {
  .hero { height: 280px; }
  .hero-text { padding: 0 24px 28px; }
  .hero-headline { font-size: 2.2rem; }
}

/* MAIN CONTENT */
.main-content {
  max-width: 1000px;
  margin: 0 auto;
  padding: 48px 32px 80px;
}

/* CONTENT CARDS */
.content-card {
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.03);
  border: 1px solid rgba(168, 85, 247, 0.3);
  border-radius: 12px;
  padding: 36px;
  margin-bottom: 32px;
  transition: border-color 0.3s ease;
}

.content-card:hover {
  border-color: rgba(168, 85, 247, 0.5);
}

.content-card h2 {
  margin-top: 0;
  margin-bottom: 20px;
  font-size: 1.9rem;
  color: #fcd34d;
  border-bottom: 2px solid rgba(250, 204, 21, 0.25);
  padding-bottom: 14px;
}

.content-card h3 {
  margin-top: 28px;
